---
title: Irish Stew (Instant Pot)
description: Brothy, potato-forward Irish stew under pressure — rump steak instead of the traditional lamb, thickened the old way by mashing potatoes into the broth.
source: Blend — see the source-blend note at the end of the file
servings: 6
time required: 1 hour 10 minutes
prep time: 15 minutes
cook time: 55 minutes
difficulty: easy
course: main
cuisine: Irish
diet: nut-free
tags: stew, irish, instant pot, beef, pressure cooker, comfort food
---

Season @rump steak{800%g}(trimmed, cut into ~4 cm chunks, patted dry) all
over with @sea salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}.

Set the #Instant Pot{} to Sauté, add @olive oil{2%tbsp} and brown the beef
in two batches for ~{7%minutes}, turning until deeply crusted. Transfer the
brown meat to a #plate{} -- no need to rest it, it goes back in soon.

Drop in @onion{2}(quartered and sliced) and Sauté for ~{4%minutes} until
soft at the edges, then add @garlic{3%cloves}(crushed) for ~{1%minute}
more — scrape the crust off the base with the garlic, splashing in stock
from the measured amount if it threatens to catch.

Add the @beef stock{750%ml}, @thyme{4%sprigs},
@bay leaves{2} and another grind of pepper. Lock the lid, valve to Sealing,
and cook on High Pressure for ~{25%minutes}. Let the pressure ease off
naturally for ~{10%minutes}, then quick-release the rest.

Nestle @potatoes{750%g}(peeled, cut into ~3 cm chunks) and
@carrots{3}(peeled, cut into ~2 cm logs) on top of the meat — don't stir
them under, they cook better sitting proud of the liquid (and adding
vegetables at a second stage keeps the potatoes from collapsing, which is
the one trick worth borrowing from pressure-cooker land). Lock the lid and
cook on High Pressure for a further ~{8%minutes}, then release naturally
for ~{10%minutes} again before opening.

Mash a few potato chunks against the side of the pot and stir the stew —
the crushed potato thickens the broth the traditional way, no flour needed.
Taste and adjust @sea salt{1%pinch}. Stir through @flat leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(roughly chopped),
saving some to scatter on top.

Serve straight from the pot in deep bowls with bread for dunking.

> Swaps to consider: for the traditional version use @?lamb shoulder{800%g} in place of the beef (same times); a bottle of @?Guinness{330%ml} replacing 330 ml of the stock makes it a dark stout stew; or @?Worcestershire sauce{2%tbsp} stirred in at the salt step adds depth without changing the character. A slurry of @?cornflour{1%tbsp} and 1 tbsp water, Sauté-polished for ~{1%minute}, replaces the mash-thicken if you want it definitively thick.

> Source blend — composition: theirishmanswife.com (classic stew, potato-mash thickener, Worcestershire note) + feastingathome.com (brothy style, herb finish). Timing: adapted to the Instant Pot from thekitchn.com (~25 min HP) and wellplated.com two-stage commenter data (27 min + 6-8 min for potatoes) — the second-stage potato addition is the conflict-resolver between authentic layers and IP mush-risk.
